+# Independent waveform transfer
+
+The receiver needs a passive Starlink observation from a distinct installation to validate raw IQ through position. A location test requires survey held outside the receiver, sample-index to UTC mapping and clock uncertainty, signed RF tuning, multiple resolvable satellite tracks at times with useful geometry, and a catalogue with capture-time availability. The public Liu et al. release provides one contiguous baseband excerpt after coarse carrier synchronization. It supplies none of the site, clock-origin, absolute RF, or multi-pass evidence required for position. This protocol tests only the original Qin receiver’s transfer to that waveform. [Capture paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.29387v2#S2) · [Publisher release](https://github.com/misaka666/Starlink-Downlink-Analysis/releases/tag/v1.0.0)
+
+## Acquisition and separation
+
+Select GitHub release v1.0.0, asset 690748124. Request bytes 0–124,999,999 once from the public 311,026,556-byte CSV. Require a matching partial-response range and the same asset size. Hash those bytes without reading past the range. Analyze exactly the first 3,600,000 I/Q pairs, or 7.5 ms at the author’s stated 480 MS/s, starting at sample zero. Fail if the range is too short. Fetch and replay are separate commands. Save source samples outside the repository. No per-range digest or standalone sample license is supplied by the author. Keep metadata and derived evidence in the repository.
+
+Use two disjoint 3.75 ms looks, labeled discovery and held. Tune the two deployed pilot centers at −115.3125 and +115.3125 MHz relative to the exported baseband center. Their nominal eight-tone bank centers are both offset another −117.1875 kHz from those tunings. Use a 1,537-tap FIR with a 4 MHz cutoff, Kaiser 8.6 and 48× decimation. Process each time look in isolation so FIR support cannot cross the split. Quantize I/Q to CI16 using a fixed 4096-counts-per-RMS scale, shared across the controls generated within each look from its target edge. The original single-receiver wide `NativeShortWindow` requires 20 ms. Place measured or control data at its start and zero-fill the remaining 16.25 ms. Report the 18.75% actual dwell support. Keep the unchanged ±800 kHz acquisition, native candidate budget, fractional output and 0.025 margin gate. Select the highest complete fractional margin per call. Neither PSS, author frame indexes nor held native outputs enter the discovery prediction.
+
+For each look and each edge pass the following seven matched inputs through the native ABI. Use a fixed seed 41821 for independent phase and symbol shuffles. Preserve detector settings across all calls.
+
+| Input | Acquisition template | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Measured target edge | Exact Qin | Main transfer |
+| Same target edge plus +171.875 kHz | Exact Qin | CFO equivariance with measured content |
+| Same target edge | Opposite edge Qin | Physical subcarrier/code specificity |
+| Same target edge | Qin with 300 active symbols permuted as blocks | Code order |
+| Same target edge with phase randomization | Exact Qin | Power-spectrum matched null |
+| Edge mixer moved 10 MHz toward center | Exact Qin | Same recording, within-channel RF control |
+| Independent synthetic full-rate target edge | Exact Qin | Known noninteger epoch and CFO |
+
+Put three synthetic frames at discovery epoch 0.18735 ms or held epoch 0.33172 ms, separated by 1/750 s. Use +137.125 kHz on the lower and −179.375 kHz on the upper edge relative to the deployed mixer. Both synthetic and measured inputs traverse the same 480 MS/s rate conversion and quantizer. Compare phase modulo the frame period, with 10 samples tolerance at 10 MS/s. Compare synthetic CFO and the known measured digital shift modulo the 1/4.4 µs symbol alias, with 1 kHz tolerance. Use the planted sample indices only for offline scoring.
+
+## Held tests
+
+Freeze the discovery winner’s phase and resolve its CFO to the strongest of the seven branches within ±800 kHz spaced by the symbol alias, using discovery IQ and the selected phase only. Use the earliest full discovery frame and all full held frames at the predicted nominal epochs. Carry the selected branch into the held score without fitting phase, frequency or clock on the held look. On those same held frames compare exact predicted coherent power with a fixed +1,387-sample epoch sham, +12,345 Hz CFO sham, opposite template and permuted template. Score the normalized squared complex matched filter on each complete range. Retain every measured point. A held panel supports transfer if its exact energy exceeds each matched sham by ≥4× in the median. Account for a missing discovery winner or a discovery gate failure instead of fitting a held substitute. Gate every phase/CFO claim using the chosen native output.
+
+Seal the native outputs and discovery predictions before estimating relative timing by a separate known full-band Humphreys PSS correlation, using the published sequence and a fixed squared-coherence threshold of 0.12. Withhold PSS timing from the native detector. Align measured windows using the first accepted PSS peak per look and retain every accepted peak to show frame spacing. This gives another waveform time mark with the same sample clock. Its absolute UTC and independent RF CFO remain unknown. [PSS sequence](https://arxiv.org/html/2210.11578#S6)
+
+The run uses at most CPUs 16 and 17, one numerical thread, and a seven-minute timeout. Record process CPU, wall, native counters, source, configuration and prediction hashes. Report denominators as one continuous exposure, two disjoint looks and two frequency lanes per look. No track, position, learned `c`, or accuracy measurement arises from this sample. Only a stage figure is eligible.
+
+Complete checks in order. Verify the source range and sample shape. Run hermetic converter, PSS and control checks. Seal native predictions. Compute PSS and held diagnostics. Verify the plotted and tabulated evidence against the seals. Reuse the installed stock detector through its single-receiver ABI. The transfer harness owns only the waveform cases, controls and evidence.
